1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,313
On May eighteenth, 2003, in the Belgian town of Schaerbeek,

2
00:00:04,313 --> 00:00:10,366
an electronic voting machine gave one candidate four thousand and ninety-six extra votes.

3
00:00:10,866 --> 00:00:14,620
It wasn't a hacker, and investigators found no software bug.

4
00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:21,439
The leading explanation is a particle from space that struck the machine's memory and flipped one single bit.

5
00:00:21,939 --> 00:00:25,494
So what happens when a cosmic ray flips a bit in your computer?

6
00:00:29,733 --> 00:00:31,748
First, the rules of the game.

7
00:00:31,748 --> 00:00:35,151
Everything your computer knows is stored as bits.

8
00:00:35,151 --> 00:00:36,193
Ones and zeros.

9
00:00:36,193 --> 00:00:39,735
A photo, a bank balance, the score in a video game.

10
00:00:39,735 --> 00:00:40,360
All bits.

11
00:00:40,860 --> 00:00:46,455
In the main memory of your computer, each bit is a tiny bucket of electric charge.

12
00:00:46,455 --> 00:00:47,410
Full is a one.

13
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:48,501
Empty is a zero.

14
00:00:49,001 --> 00:00:50,721
And modern buckets are tiny.

15
00:00:50,721 --> 00:00:52,994
It doesn't take much to tip one over.

16
00:00:53,833 --> 00:00:55,274
Bits also have values.

17
00:00:55,274 --> 00:00:58,351
In a binary number, the first bit is worth one.

18
00:00:58,351 --> 00:01:02,934
The next is worth two, then four, eight, sixteen, doubling every time.

19
00:01:03,434 --> 00:01:06,333
So flipping a low bit barely changes a number.

20
00:01:06,333 --> 00:01:10,176
But the thirteenth bit is worth four thousand and ninety-six.

21
00:01:10,176 --> 00:01:11,499
Remember that number.

22
00:01:14,233 --> 00:01:16,337
So where does the hit come from?

23
00:01:16,337 --> 00:01:19,559
Cosmic rays are mostly protons and atomic nuclei,

24
00:01:19,559 --> 00:01:23,110
racing across the galaxy at nearly the speed of light.

25
00:01:23,110 --> 00:01:26,529
Many are thought to be flung out by exploding stars.

26
00:01:27,029 --> 00:01:29,834
When one slams into the top of our atmosphere,

27
00:01:29,834 --> 00:01:32,577
it shatters into a shower of other particles,

28
00:01:32,577 --> 00:01:35,259
spreading out as it rains toward the ground.

29
00:01:35,759 --> 00:01:37,657
They were discovered in 1912,

30
00:01:37,657 --> 00:01:42,566
when the physicist Victor Hess carried radiation detectors up in a balloon,

31
00:01:42,566 --> 00:01:44,595
more than five kilometers high,

32
00:01:44,595 --> 00:01:48,260
and found the radiation got stronger the higher he went.

33
00:01:48,260 --> 00:01:48,980
Not weaker.

34
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:50,616
It was coming from space.

35
00:01:51,116 --> 00:01:54,417
The most energetic one ever recorded, in 1991,

36
00:01:54,417 --> 00:02:00,444
carried as much energy as a baseball thrown at about ninety-five kilometers an hour.

37
00:02:00,444 --> 00:02:04,391
All packed into a single particle smaller than an atom.

38
00:02:04,391 --> 00:02:07,763
Scientists nicknamed it the Oh-My-God particle.

39
00:02:08,263 --> 00:02:08,864
Right now,

40
00:02:08,864 --> 00:02:13,671
about one muon from those showers passes through every square centimeter of you,

41
00:02:13,671 --> 00:02:14,452
every minute.

42
00:02:14,452 --> 00:02:17,215
Through your body, your phone and your laptop.

43
00:02:18,067 --> 00:02:19,635
But for chips on the ground,

44
00:02:19,635 --> 00:02:22,883
the main troublemakers are neutrons from the same showers.

45
00:02:23,383 --> 00:02:25,797
And the higher you go, the worse it gets.

46
00:02:25,797 --> 00:02:27,975
At the height where airliners cruise,

47
00:02:27,975 --> 00:02:32,273
the neutron flux is about three hundred times stronger than at sea level.

48
00:02:32,773 --> 00:02:38,084
That's why the computers that fly airliners have to be designed with these hits in mind.

49
00:02:38,084 --> 00:02:43,757
And it's why a computer in a mountain town gets hit more often than the same one at the beach.

50
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:48,966
When a particle hits a chip just right,

51
00:02:48,966 --> 00:02:52,570
it leaves a thin trail of electric charge in the silicon.

52
00:02:52,570 --> 00:02:57,059
If that trail lands next to a memory cell, it can fill an empty bucket,

53
00:02:57,059 --> 00:02:58,324
or drain a full one.

54
00:02:58,824 --> 00:03:00,276
A zero becomes a one.

55
00:03:00,276 --> 00:03:04,149
Engineers call it a soft error, or a single-event upset.

56
00:03:04,149 --> 00:03:05,394
Nothing is broken.

57
00:03:05,394 --> 00:03:09,129
The memory just remembers something it was never told.

58
00:03:09,629 --> 00:03:12,112
Most of the time, you'd never notice.

59
00:03:12,112 --> 00:03:15,199
The flipped bit sits in memory nobody's using,

60
00:03:15,199 --> 00:03:17,749
or it gets overwritten a moment later.

61
00:03:17,749 --> 00:03:19,628
Sometimes a program crashes.

62
00:03:19,628 --> 00:03:22,312
And sometimes, a number quietly changes.

63
00:03:22,812 --> 00:03:26,956
And a single particle can even flip several neighboring bits at once.

64
00:03:26,956 --> 00:03:28,638
That's much harder to catch,

65
00:03:28,638 --> 00:03:32,121
because many protection schemes are built to fix just one.

66
00:03:34,867 --> 00:03:35,892
Back to Belgium.

67
00:03:35,892 --> 00:03:40,891
Officials noticed that the candidate's total didn't add up, so they recounted.

68
00:03:40,891 --> 00:03:44,352
The four thousand and ninety-six extra votes vanished.

69
00:03:44,852 --> 00:03:48,580
Four thousand and ninety-six is two to the twelfth power,

70
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:50,739
the value of that thirteenth bit.

71
00:03:50,739 --> 00:03:53,290
Exactly what one flipped bit would add.

72
00:03:53,290 --> 00:03:56,102
After ruling out fraud and software errors,

73
00:03:56,102 --> 00:04:00,747
investigators concluded a single-event upset was the most likely cause.

74
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:03,439
Out in space, it's far worse.

75
00:04:03,439 --> 00:04:06,484
Over a region called the South Atlantic Anomaly,

76
00:04:06,484 --> 00:04:09,528
Earth's radiation belts dip close to the planet.

77
00:04:09,528 --> 00:04:13,778
The Hubble Space Telescope passes through it on many of its orbits,

78
00:04:13,778 --> 00:04:17,330
and doesn't take science observations while it's inside.

79
00:04:17,830 --> 00:04:20,083
In 2010, the Voyager 2 spacecraft,

80
00:04:20,083 --> 00:04:23,329
more than thirteen billion kilometers from Earth,

81
00:04:23,329 --> 00:04:25,979
suddenly started sending home gibberish.

82
00:04:26,479 --> 00:04:32,304
Engineers traced it to a single bit in its data computer that had flipped from a zero to a one.

83
00:04:32,304 --> 00:04:36,413
They sent a command to flip it back, and Voyager went back to work.

84
00:04:36,913 --> 00:04:39,096
Nobody can prove what flipped it.

85
00:04:39,096 --> 00:04:42,138
But out there, radiation is the usual suspect.

86
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,859
And in 2013, during a livestream of Super Mario 64,

87
00:04:46,859 --> 00:04:50,945
a player's Mario suddenly shot up onto a higher floor,

88
00:04:50,945 --> 00:04:55,106
a jump nobody has been able to repeat in a normal game.

89
00:04:55,606 --> 00:04:56,697
A popular theory:

90
00:04:56,697 --> 00:04:59,584
a single bit in Mario's height value flipped.

91
00:04:59,584 --> 00:05:03,049
It's never been proven, and fans still argue about it.

92
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:07,123
The first chip culprits weren't even from space.

93
00:05:07,123 --> 00:05:07,660
In 1978,

94
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:14,441
Intel engineers Timothy May and Murray Woods found that memory chips were flipping bits on their own.

95
00:05:14,941 --> 00:05:15,626
The cause:

96
00:05:15,626 --> 00:05:19,943
tiny traces of uranium and thorium in the chips' own packaging,

97
00:05:19,943 --> 00:05:21,793
giving off alpha particles.

98
00:05:21,793 --> 00:05:25,014
Chip makers had to start using purer materials.

99
00:05:25,867 --> 00:05:29,828
Bit flips can even send your computer to the wrong website.

100
00:05:29,828 --> 00:05:30,365
In 2011,

101
00:05:30,365 --> 00:05:37,617
security researcher Artem Dinaburg registered web addresses that were exactly one bit away from famous ones.

102
00:05:38,117 --> 00:05:39,490
Over about seven months,

103
00:05:39,490 --> 00:05:42,465
they received more than fifty-two thousand requests,

104
00:05:42,465 --> 00:05:46,012
from computers that had flipped a bit somewhere along the way.

105
00:05:48,767 --> 00:05:50,922
So how often does this happen?

106
00:05:50,922 --> 00:05:51,856
In the 1990s,

107
00:05:51,856 --> 00:05:59,686
IBM estimated roughly one cosmic-ray error per month for every two hundred and fifty-six megabytes of memory.

108
00:05:59,686 --> 00:06:02,201
Other estimates came out far lower.

109
00:06:02,701 --> 00:06:03,240
In 2009,

110
00:06:03,240 --> 00:06:10,716
a study of Google's servers found that more than eight percent of memory modules had at least one error a year.

111
00:06:10,716 --> 00:06:16,375
But the researchers argued that many came from worn or faulty chips, not from space.

112
00:06:16,875 --> 00:06:17,711
And in 2026,

113
00:06:17,711 --> 00:06:24,052
a Mozilla engineer estimated that roughly one in ten Firefox crashes looked like bit flips,

114
00:06:24,052 --> 00:06:26,003
most likely from bad memory.

115
00:06:26,003 --> 00:06:31,159
So sometimes, when software crashes, it really isn't the software's fault.

116
00:06:33,900 --> 00:06:36,858
So why doesn't your computer go haywire every day?

117
00:06:36,858 --> 00:06:38,573
Because engineers fight back.

118
00:06:39,073 --> 00:06:39,612
In 1950,

119
00:06:39,612 --> 00:06:46,887
Richard Hamming at Bell Labs got fed up with a relay computer that kept stopping on errors over the weekend,

120
00:06:46,887 --> 00:06:48,032
wasting his work.

121
00:06:48,032 --> 00:06:50,592
So he invented error-correcting codes.

122
00:06:51,092 --> 00:06:51,667
The idea:

123
00:06:51,667 --> 00:06:54,794
store a few extra check bits alongside your data.

124
00:06:54,794 --> 00:06:59,518
If one bit flips, the check bits can tell you which one, and flip it back.

125
00:07:00,367 --> 00:07:02,507
Servers use this in ECC memory.

126
00:07:02,507 --> 00:07:04,371
Spacecraft go even further.

127
00:07:04,371 --> 00:07:10,791
The Cassini probe's data recorders stored seven check bits for every thirty-two bits of data,

128
00:07:10,791 --> 00:07:14,796
and swept their memory clean of errors every nine minutes.

129
00:07:15,296 --> 00:07:20,212
Some systems simply run the same computer several times over and let them vote.

130
00:07:20,212 --> 00:07:23,199
The Space Shuttle flew with five main computers,

131
00:07:23,199 --> 00:07:26,995
four of them running the same software and comparing answers.

132
00:07:27,833 --> 00:07:29,147
And here's the twist.

133
00:07:29,147 --> 00:07:31,400
Those same cosmic rays help us, too.

134
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:36,112
Chip makers test their hardware by firing beams of neutrons at it,

135
00:07:36,112 --> 00:07:39,367
cramming years of cosmic-ray hits into a few hours.

136
00:07:39,867 --> 00:07:44,599
And in 2017, scientists used muons from cosmic rays like an X-ray,

137
00:07:44,599 --> 00:07:47,539
to look inside the Great Pyramid of Giza.

138
00:07:47,539 --> 00:07:52,271
They found a huge hidden empty space, at least thirty meters long,

139
00:07:52,271 --> 00:07:54,207
that nobody knew was there.

140
00:07:55,067 --> 00:07:57,055
So right now, as you watch this,

141
00:07:57,055 --> 00:08:01,466
particles from distant exploding stars are passing through your screen.

142
00:08:01,466 --> 00:08:02,957
Almost all of them miss.

143
00:08:02,957 --> 00:08:06,249
And when one doesn't, your computer quietly fixes it.

144
00:08:06,249 --> 00:08:08,859
Or it crashes, and you blame the software.

145
00:08:09,359 --> 00:08:12,837
Have you ever had a glitch so weird it might have been a cosmic ray?

146
00:08:12,837 --> 00:08:14,065
Tell me in the comments.
